From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Phononenom <64574232+Phononenom@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 12:44:59 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: the RADV driver on mihawk's
 PS5_Mesa 75238ae4, with memory compaction

tools/deps.json moves ps5-mesa (https://github.com/mihawk-99/PS5_Mesa) from 7b59ef27 to 75238ae4. The
build's patches to it: RADV_PS5_COMPACT, which packs the driver's partly used memory blocks so whole ones
go back to the pool (tools/radv-compact.inc, radv-compact-plan.inc, checked by check-radv-compact.py);
shaders the driver refused handled (patch-radv-refused-shaders.py); the trim, pieces, WSI and robustness
patches on the new pin.

From these commits (oldest first):
- 2026-11-18 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: the driver maps sparse ranges for the GPU only
- 2026-11-18 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: the driver's trim never gives back memory a sparse resource shows
- 2026-11-18 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: the driver watches its shared zero block and clears it when written
- 2026-11-18 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: each large sparse resource has a zero block of its own
- 2026-11-18 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: the driver's image and buffer counting is patched once a build
- 2026-11-18 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: a sparse bind with no memory leaves its pieces unbound
- 2026-11-19 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: the driver logs the memory it frees while memory is short
- 2026-11-19 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: driver memory compaction, a dry run and a self-test
- 2026-11-19 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: driver memory compaction moves memory (RADV_PS5_COMPACT=1)
- 2026-11-19 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: driver memory compaction on by default; the free trace out
- 2026-11-19 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: compaction keeps CPU page tables free
- 2026-11-19 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: the PS5 driver moves to Mesa 75238ae
- 2026-11-19 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: a pipeline whose shader was refused memory fails, not missing a stage
- 2026-11-19 Playtendoh's Switch 2 side: the driver's free trace back, where memory is really unmapped
